{
  "module": "Input side: text to vectors",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "tokens",
    "title": "Tokens and the vocabulary",
    "summary": "How a language model turns text into the numbers it actually reads: tokens, the vocabulary, token IDs and the tokenizer.",
    "covers": ["token", "vocabulary", "token-id", "tokenizer"],
    "prerequisites": []
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "reads",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 1-4)"
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A language model does not read letters or words the way you do. It reads a sequence of "
            },
            {
              "text": "tokens",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Everything that follows in this course starts from this fact: the model's input is a list of tokens, never raw text."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "token",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 1, 22)"
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A "
            },
            {
              "text": "token",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " is the unit of text the model reads and writes. Depending on how the text is split, a token can be a whole word, a piece of a word, a punctuation mark, or even a few raw bytes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A common English word is usually a single token. A rare word is usually split into several pieces."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "vocab",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, card 2)"
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The "
            },
            {
              "text": "vocabulary",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " is the complete, fixed list of tokens the model can read and write. Its size is written "
            },
            {
              "text": "V",
              "code": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Real models use vocabularies of tens of thousands to a few hundred thousand tokens. Llama 2 has 32,000; GPT-2 has 50,257."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "ids",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, card 3)"
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Every token has a position in the vocabulary, numbered from 0 up to "
            },
            {
              "text": "V - 1",
              "code": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". That number is the token's "
            },
            {
              "text": "ID",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A piece of text therefore becomes a list of integers, one per token. That list of integers is the only thing the network receives about the text."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "tokenizer",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, card 4)"
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The "
            },
            {
              "text": "tokenizer",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " is the program that converts between text and token IDs. Encoding turns text into IDs; decoding turns IDs back into text."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It is a fixed, deterministic program: the same text always gives the same IDs."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "What does the network actually receive as its input?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "correct": true,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "A list of integers, the token IDs"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The network is only ever given the IDs of the tokens; the text itself never reaches it."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The original characters of the text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Characters are never given to the network. The text is converted to token IDs first."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "A list of whole words"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Words are not the unit. The input is tokens, and a token can be a piece of a word."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "A picture of the text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "A language model reads a sequence of integers, not an image."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "teaches": ["reads", "ids"],
      "covers": ["token-id"],
      "variants": [
        {
          "prompt": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
